.SH "PCRE2 SPECIAL CATEGORY PROPERTIES FOR \(rsp and \(rsP"
.sp
.ti +2
Xan        Alphanumeric: union of properties L and N
.br
.ti +2
Xps        POSIX space: property Z or tab, NL, VT, FF, CR
.br
.ti +2
Xsp        Perl space: property Z or tab, NL, VT, FF, CR
.br
.ti +2
Xuc        Universally\-named character: one that can be
.br
.ti +2
             represented by a Universal Character Name
.br
.ti +2
Xwd        Perl word: property Xan or underscore
.sp
Perl and POSIX space are now the same. Perl added VT to its space character set
at release 5.18.
.SH "BINARY PROPERTIES FOR \(rsp AND \(rsP"
.sp
Unicode defines a number of binary properties, that is, properties whose only
values are true or false. You can obtain a list of those that are recognized by
\(rsp and \(rsP, along with their abbreviations, by running this command:
.sp
.ti +2
pcre2test \-LP
.SH "SCRIPT MATCHING WITH \(rsp AND \(rsP"
.sp
Many script names and their 4\-letter abbreviations are recognized in
\(rsp{sc:...} or \(rsp{scx:...} items, or on their own with \(rsp (and also \(rsP of
course). You can obtain a list of these scripts by running this command:
.sp
.ti +2
pcre2test \-LS
.SH "THE BIDI_CLASS PROPERTY FOR \(rsp AND \(rsP"
.sp
.ti +2
\(rsp{Bidi_Class:<class>}   matches a character with the given class
.br
.ti +2
\(rsp{BC:<class>}           matches a character with the given class
.sp
The recognized classes are:
.sp
.ti +2
AL          Arabic letter
.br
.ti +2
AN          Arabic number
.br
.ti +2
B           paragraph separator
.br
.ti +2
BN          boundary neutral
.br
.ti +2
CS          common separator
.br
.ti +2
EN          European number
.br
.ti +2
ES          European separator
.br
.ti +2
ET          European terminator
.br
.ti +2
FSI         first strong isolate
.br
.ti +2
L           left\-to\-right
.br
.ti +2
LRE         left\-to\-right embedding
.br
.ti +2
LRI         left\-to\-right isolate
.br
.ti +2
LRO         left\-to\-right override
.br
.ti +2
NSM         non\-spacing mark
.br
.ti +2
ON          other neutral
.br
.ti +2
PDF         pop directional format
.br
.ti +2
PDI         pop directional isolate
.br
.ti +2
R           right\-to\-left
.br
.ti +2
RLE         right\-to\-left embedding
.br
.ti +2
RLI         right\-to\-left isolate
.br
.ti +2
RLO         right\-to\-left override
.br
.ti +2
S           segment separator
.br
.ti +2
WS          white space
.SH "CHARACTER CLASSES"
.sp
.ti +2
[...]       positive character class
.br
.ti +2
[^...]      negative character class
.br
.ti +2
[x\-y]       range (can be used for hex characters)
.br
.ti +2
[[:xxx:]]   positive POSIX named set
.br
.ti +2
[[:^xxx:]]  negative POSIX named set
.sp
.ti +2
alnum       alphanumeric
.br
.ti +2
alpha       alphabetic
.br
.ti +2
ascii       0\-127
.br
.ti +2
blank       space or tab
.br
.ti +2
cntrl       control character
.br
.ti +2
digit       decimal digit
.br
.ti +2
graph       printing, excluding space
.br
.ti +2
lower       lower case letter
.br
.ti +2
print       printing, including space
.br
.ti +2
punct       printing, excluding alphanumeric
.br
.ti +2
space       white space
.br
.ti +2
upper       upper case letter
.br
.ti +2
word        same as \(rsw
.br
.ti +2
xdigit      hexadecimal digit
.sp
In PCRE2, POSIX character set names recognize only ASCII characters by default,
but some of them use Unicode properties if PCRE2_UCP is set. You can use
\(rsQ...\(rsE inside a character class.
.sp
When PCRE2_ALT_EXTENDED_CLASS is set, UTS#18 extended character classes may be
used, allowing nested character classes, combined using set operators.
.sp
.ti +2
[x&&[^y]]   UTS#18 extended character class
.sp
.ti +2
x||y        set union (OR)
.br
.ti +2
x&&y        set intersection (AND)
.br
.ti +2
x\-\-y        set difference (AND NOT)
.br
.ti +2
x~~y        set symmetric difference (XOR)
.SH "PERL EXTENDED CHARACTER CLASSES"
.sp
.ti +2
(?[...])                Perl extended character class
.br
.ti +2
(?[\(rsp{Thai} & \(rsp{Nd}])  operators; white space ignored
.br
.ti +2
(?[(x \- y) & z])        parentheses for grouping
.sp
.ti +2
(?[ [^3] & \(rsp{Nd} ])    [...] is a nested ordinary class
.br
.ti +2
(?[ [:alpha:] \- [z] ])  POSIX set is allowed outside [...]
.br
.ti +2
(?[ \(rsd \- [3] ])         backslash\-escaped set is allowed outside [...]
.br
.ti +2
(?[ !\(rsn & [:ascii:] ])  backslash\-escaped character is allowed outside [...]
.br
.ti +2
                    all other characters or ranges must be enclosed in [...]
.sp
.ti +2
x|y, x+y                set union (OR)
.br
.ti +2
x&y                     set intersection (AND)
.br
.ti +2
x\-y                     set difference (AND NOT)
.br
.ti +2
x^y                     set symmetric difference (XOR)
.br
.ti +2
!x                      set complement (NOT)
.sp
Inside a Perl extended character class, [...] switches mode to be interpreted
as an ordinary character class. Outside of a nested [...], the only items
permitted are backslash\-escapes, POSIX sets, operators, and parentheses. Inside
a nested ordinary class, ^ has its usual meaning (inverts the class when used
as the first character); outside of a nested class, ^ is the XOR operator.
.SH "QUANTIFIERS"
.sp
.ti +2
?           0 or 1, greedy
.br
.ti +2
?+          0 or 1, possessive
.br
.ti +2
??          0 or 1, lazy
.br
.ti +2
*           0 or more, greedy
.br
.ti +2
*+          0 or more, possessive
.br
.ti +2
*?          0 or more, lazy
.br
.ti +2
+           1 or more, greedy
.br
.ti +2
++          1 or more, possessive
.br
.ti +2
+?          1 or more, lazy
.br
.ti +2
{n}         exactly n
.br
.ti +2
{n,m}       at least n, no more than m, greedy
.br
.ti +2
{n,m}+      at least n, no more than m, possessive
.br
.ti +2
{n,m}?      at least n, no more than m, lazy
.br
.ti +2
{n,}        n or more, greedy
.br
.ti +2
{n,}+       n or more, possessive
.br
.ti +2
{n,}?       n or more, lazy
.br
.ti +2
{,m}        zero up to m, greedy
.br
.ti +2
{,m}+       zero up to m, possessive
.br
.ti +2
{,m}?       zero up to m, lazy
.SH "ANCHORS AND SIMPLE ASSERTIONS"
.sp
.ti +2
\(rsb          word boundary
.br
.ti +2
\(rsB          not a word boundary
.br
.ti +2
^           start of subject
.br
.ti +2
              also after an internal newline in multiline mode
.br
.ti +2
              (after any newline if PCRE2_ALT_CIRCUMFLEX is set)
.br
.ti +2
\(rsA          start of subject
.br
.ti +2
$           end of subject
.br
.ti +2
              also before newline at end of subject
.br
.ti +2
              also before internal newline in multiline mode
.br
.ti +2
\(rsZ          end of subject
.br
.ti +2
              also before newline at end of subject
.br
.ti +2
\(rsz          end of subject
.br
.ti +2
\(rsG          first matching position in subject
.SH "REPORTED MATCH POINT SETTING"
.sp
.ti +2
\(rsK          set reported start of match
.sp
From release 10.38 \(rsK is not permitted by default in lookaround assertions,
for compatibility with Perl. However, if the PCRE2_EXTRA_ALLOW_LOOKAROUND_BSK
option is set, the previous behaviour is re\-enabled. When this option is set,
\(rsK is honoured in positive assertions, but ignored in negative ones.
.SH "ALTERNATION"
.sp
.ti +2
expr|expr|expr...
.SH "CAPTURING"
.sp
.ti +2
(...)           capture group
.br
.ti +2
(?<name>...)    named capture group (Perl)
.br
.ti +2
(?\*(Aqname\*(Aq...)    named capture group (Perl)
.br
.ti +2
(?P<name>...)   named capture group (Python)
.br
.ti +2
(?:...)         non\-capture group
.br
.ti +2
(?|...)         non\-capture group; reset group numbers for
.br
.ti +2
                 capture groups in each alternative
.sp
In non\-UTF modes, names may contain underscores and ASCII letters and digits;
in UTF modes, any Unicode letters and Unicode decimal digits are permitted. In
both cases, a name must not start with a digit.
.SH "ATOMIC GROUPS"
.sp
.ti +2
(?>...)         atomic non\-capture group
.br
.ti +2
(*atomic:...)   atomic non\-capture group
.SH "COMMENT"
.sp
.ti +2
(?#....)        comment (not nestable)
.SH "OPTION SETTING"
.sp
Changes of these options within a group are automatically cancelled at the end
of the group.
.sp
.ti +2
(?a)            all ASCII options
.br
.ti +2
(?aD)           restrict \(rsd to ASCII in UCP mode
.br
.ti +2
(?aS)           restrict \(rss to ASCII in UCP mode
.br
.ti +2
(?aW)           restrict \(rsw to ASCII in UCP mode
.br
.ti +2
(?aP)           restrict all POSIX classes to ASCII in UCP mode
.br
.ti +2
(?aT)           restrict POSIX digit classes to ASCII in UCP mode
.br
.ti +2
(?i)            caseless
.br
.ti +2
(?J)            allow duplicate named groups
.br
.ti +2
(?m)            multiline
.br
.ti +2
(?n)            no auto capture
.br
.ti +2
(?r)            restrict caseless to either ASCII or non\-ASCII
.br
.ti +2
(?s)            single line (dotall)
.br
.ti +2
(?U)            default ungreedy (lazy)
.br
.ti +2
(?x)            ignore white space except in classes or \(rsQ...\(rsE
.br
.ti +2
(?xx)           as (?x) but also ignore space and tab in classes
.br
.ti +2
(?\-...)         unset the given option(s)
.br
.ti +2
(?^)            unset imnrsx options
.sp
(?aP) implies (?aT) as well, though this has no additional effect. However, it
means that (?\-aP) also implies (?\-aT) and disables all ASCII restrictions for
POSIX classes.
.sp
Unsetting x or xx unsets both. Several options may be set at once, and a
mixture of setting and unsetting such as (?i\-x) is allowed, but there may be
only one hyphen. Setting (but no unsetting) is allowed after (?^ for example
(?^in). An option setting may appear at the start of a non\-capture group, for
example (?i:...).
.sp
The following are recognized only at the very start of a pattern or after one
of the newline or \(rsR sequences or options with similar syntax. More than one
of them may appear. For the first three, d is a decimal number.
.sp
.ti +2
(*LIMIT_DEPTH=d)     set the backtracking limit to d
.br
.ti +2
(*LIMIT_HEAP=d)      set the heap size limit to d * 1024 bytes
.br
.ti +2
(*LIMIT_MATCH=d)     set the match limit to d
.br
.ti +2
(*CASELESS_RESTRICT) set PCRE2_EXTRA_CASELESS_RESTRICT when matching
.br
.ti +2
(*NOTEMPTY)          set PCRE2_NOTEMPTY when matching
.br
.ti +2
(*NOTEMPTY_ATSTART)  set PCRE2_NOTEMPTY_ATSTART when matching
.br
.ti +2
(*NO_AUTO_POSSESS)   no auto\-possessification (PCRE2_NO_AUTO_POSSESS)
.br
.ti +2
(*NO_DOTSTAR_ANCHOR) no .* anchoring (PCRE2_NO_DOTSTAR_ANCHOR)
.br
.ti +2
(*NO_JIT)            disable JIT optimization
.br
.ti +2
(*NO_START_OPT)      no start\-match optimization (PCRE2_NO_START_OPTIMIZE)
.br
.ti +2
(*TURKISH_CASING)    set PCRE2_EXTRA_TURKISH_CASING when matching
.br
.ti +2
(*UTF)               set appropriate UTF mode for the library in use
.br
.ti +2
(*UCP)               set PCRE2_UCP (use Unicode properties for \(rsd etc)
.sp
Note that LIMIT_DEPTH, LIMIT_HEAP, and LIMIT_MATCH can only reduce the value of
the limits set by the caller of \f(CRpcre2_match()\fP or \f(CRpcre2_dfa_match()\fP,
not increase them. LIMIT_RECURSION is an obsolete synonym for LIMIT_DEPTH. The
application can lock out the use of (*UTF) and (*UCP) by setting the
PCRE2_NEVER_UTF or PCRE2_NEVER_UCP options, respectively, at compile time.
.SH "NEWLINE CONVENTION"
.sp
These are recognized only at the very start of the pattern or after option
settings with a similar syntax.
.sp
.ti +2
(*CR)           carriage return only
.br
.ti +2
(*LF)           linefeed only
.br
.ti +2
(*CRLF)         carriage return followed by linefeed
.br
.ti +2
(*ANYCRLF)      all three of the above
.br
.ti +2
(*ANY)          any Unicode newline sequence
.br
.ti +2
(*NUL)          the NUL character (binary zero)
.SH "WHAT \(rsR MATCHES"
.sp
These are recognized only at the very start of the pattern or after option
setting with a similar syntax.
.sp
.ti +2
(*BSR_ANYCRLF)  CR, LF, or CRLF
.br
.ti +2
(*BSR_UNICODE)  any Unicode newline sequence
.SH "LOOKAHEAD AND LOOKBEHIND ASSERTIONS"
.sp
.ti +2
(?=...)                     )
.br
.ti +2
(*pla:...)                  ) positive lookahead
.br
.ti +2
(*positive_lookahead:...)   )
.sp
.ti +2
(?!...)                     )
.br
.ti +2
(*nla:...)                  ) negative lookahead
.br
.ti +2
(*negative_lookahead:...)   )
.sp
.ti +2
(?<=...)                    )
.br
.ti +2
(*plb:...)                  ) positive lookbehind
.br
.ti +2
(*positive_lookbehind:...)  )
.sp
.ti +2
(?<!...)                    )
.br
.ti +2
(*nlb:...)                  ) negative lookbehind
.br
.ti +2
(*negative_lookbehind:...)  )
.sp
Each top\-level branch of a lookbehind must have a limit for the number of
characters it matches. If any branch can match a variable number of characters,
the maximum for each branch is limited to a value set by the caller of
\f(CRpcre2_compile()\fP or defaulted. The default is set when PCRE2 is built
(ultimate default 255). If every branch matches a fixed number of characters,
the limit for each branch is 65535 characters.
.SH "NON\-ATOMIC LOOKAROUND ASSERTIONS"
.sp
These assertions are specific to PCRE2 and are not Perl\-compatible.
.sp
.ti +2
(?*...)                                )
.br
.ti +2
(*napla:...)                           ) synonyms
.br
.ti +2
(*non_atomic_positive_lookahead:...)   )
.sp
.ti +2
(?<*...)                               )
.br
.ti +2
(*naplb:...)                           ) synonyms
.br
.ti +2
(*non_atomic_positive_lookbehind:...)  )
.SH "SUBSTRING SCAN ASSERTION"
.sp
This feature is not Perl\-compatible.
.sp
.ti +2
(*scan_substring:(grouplist)...)  scan captured substring
.br
.ti +2
(*scs:(grouplist)...)             scan captured substring
.sp
The comma\-separated list "grouplist" may identify groups in any of the
following ways:
.sp
.ti +2
n       absolute reference
.br
.ti +2
+n      relative reference
.br
.ti +2
\-n      relative reference
.br
.ti +2
<name>  name
.br
.ti +2
\*(Aqname\*(Aq  name
.SH "SCRIPT RUNS"
.sp
.ti +2
(*script_run:...)           ) script run, can be backtracked into
.br
.ti +2
(*sr:...)                   )
.sp
.ti +2
(*atomic_script_run:...)    ) atomic script run
.br
.ti +2
(*asr:...)                  )
.SH "BACKREFERENCES"
.sp
.ti +2
\(rsn              reference by number (can be ambiguous)
.br
.ti +2
\(rsgn             reference by number
.br
.ti +2
\(rsg{n}           reference by number
.br
.ti +2
\(rsg+n            relative reference by number (PCRE2 extension)
.br
.ti +2
\(rsg\-n            relative reference by number
.br
.ti +2
\(rsg{+n}          relative reference by number (PCRE2 extension)
.br
.ti +2
\(rsg{\-n}          relative reference by number
.br
.ti +2
\(rsk<name>        reference by name (Perl)
.br
.ti +2
\(rsk\*(Aqname\*(Aq        reference by name (Perl)
.br
.ti +2
\(rsg{name}        reference by name (Perl)
.br
.ti +2
\(rsk{name}        reference by name (.NET)
.br
.ti +2
(?P=name)       reference by name (Python)
.SH "SUBROUTINE REFERENCES (POSSIBLY RECURSIVE)"
.sp
.ti +2
(?R)            recurse whole pattern
.br
.ti +2
(?n)            call subroutine by absolute number
.br
.ti +2
(?+n)           call subroutine by relative number
.br
.ti +2
(?\-n)           call subroutine by relative number
.br
.ti +2
(?&name)        call subroutine by name (Perl)
.br
.ti +2
(?P>name)       call subroutine by name (Python)
.br
.ti +2
\(rsg<name>        call subroutine by name (Oniguruma)
.br
.ti +2
\(rsg\*(Aqname\*(Aq        call subroutine by name (Oniguruma)
.br
.ti +2
\(rsg<n>           call subroutine by absolute number (Oniguruma)
.br
.ti +2
\(rsg\*(Aqn\*(Aq           call subroutine by absolute number (Oniguruma)
.br
.ti +2
\(rsg<+n>          call subroutine by relative number (PCRE2 extension)
.br
.ti +2
\(rsg\*(Aq+n\*(Aq          call subroutine by relative number (PCRE2 extension)
.br
.ti +2
\(rsg<\-n>          call subroutine by relative number (PCRE2 extension)
.br
.ti +2
\(rsg\*(Aq\-n\*(Aq          call subroutine by relative number (PCRE2 extension)
.sp
The variants using parentheses (?...) may also specify a list of capture groups
to return, which shall be retained in the calling subexpression if set during
the recursion (this feature is not supported by Perl).
.sp
.ti +2
(?R(grouplist))       recurse whole pattern, returning capture groups
.br
.ti +2
                        (PCRE2 extension)
.br
.ti +2
(?n(grouplist))       )
.br
.ti +2
(?+n(grouplist))      ) call subroutine, returning capture groups
.br
.ti +2
(?\-n(grouplist))      )   (PCRE2 extension)
.br
.ti +2
(?&name(grouplist))   )
.br
.ti +2
(?P>name(grouplist))  )
.sp
The comma\-separated list "grouplist" uses the same syntax as
(*scan_substring:(grouplist)...), and may identify groups in any of the
following ways:
.sp
.ti +2
n       absolute reference
.br
.ti +2
+n      relative reference
.br
.ti +2
\-n      relative reference
.br
.ti +2
<name>  name
.br
.ti +2
\*(Aqname\*(Aq  name
.SH "CONDITIONAL PATTERNS"
.sp
.ti +2
(?(condition)yes\-pattern)
.br
.ti +2
(?(condition)yes\-pattern|no\-pattern)
.sp
.ti +2
(?(n)                absolute reference condition
.br
.ti +2
(?(+n)               relative reference condition (PCRE2 extension)
.br
.ti +2
(?(\-n)               relative reference condition (PCRE2 extension)
.br
.ti +2
(?(<name>)           named reference condition (Perl)
.br
.ti +2
(?(\*(Aqname\*(Aq)           named reference condition (Perl)
.br
.ti +2
(?(name)             named reference condition (PCRE2, deprecated)
.br
.ti +2
(?(R)                overall recursion condition
.br
.ti +2
(?(Rn)               specific numbered group recursion condition
.br
.ti +2
(?(R&name)           specific named group recursion condition
.br
.ti +2
(?(DEFINE)           define groups for reference
.br
.ti +2
(?(VERSION[>]=n[.m]) test PCRE2 version
.br
.ti +2
(?(assert)           assertion condition
.sp
Note the ambiguity of (?\(rg and (?(Rn) which might be named reference
conditions or recursion tests. Such a condition is interpreted as a reference
condition if the relevant named group exists.
.sp
The parts within brackets for the VERSION conditional syntax could be ommited.
The fractional part of the version number defaults to 0 in that case.
.SH "BACKTRACKING CONTROL"
.sp
All backtracking control verbs may be in the form (*VERB:NAME). For (*MARK) the
name is mandatory, for the others it is optional. (*SKIP) changes its behaviour
if :NAME is present. The others just set a name for passing back to the caller,
but this is not a name that (*SKIP) can see. The following act immediately they
are reached:
.sp
.ti +2
(*ACCEPT)       force successful match
.br
.ti +2
(*FAIL)         force backtrack; synonym (*F)
.br
.ti +2
(*MARK:NAME)    set name to be passed back; synonym (*:NAME)
.sp
The following act only when a subsequent match failure causes a backtrack to
reach them. They all force a match failure, but they differ in what happens
afterwards. Those that advance the start\-of\-match point do so only if the
pattern is not anchored.
.sp
.ti +2
(*COMMIT)       overall failure, no advance of starting point
.br
.ti +2
(*PRUNE)        advance to next starting character
.br
.ti +2
(*SKIP)         advance to current matching position
.br
.ti +2
(*SKIP:NAME)    advance to position corresponding to an earlier
.br
.ti +2
                (*MARK:NAME); if not found, the (*SKIP) is ignored
.br
.ti +2
(*THEN)         local failure, backtrack to next alternation
.sp
The effect of one of these verbs in a group called as a subroutine is confined
to the subroutine call.
.SH "CALLOUTS"
.sp
.ti +2
(?C)            callout (assumed number 0)
.br
.ti +2
(?Cn)           callout with numerical data n
.br
.ti +2
(?C"text")      callout with string data
.sp
The allowed string delimiters are ` \*(Aq " ^ % # $ (which are the same for the
start and the end), and the starting delimiter { matched with the ending
delimiter }. To encode the ending delimiter within the string, double it.
.SH "REPLACEMENT STRINGS"
.sp
If the PCRE2_SUBSTITUTE_LITERAL option is set, a replacement string for
\f(CRpcre2_substitute()\fP is not interpreted. Otherwise, by default, the only
special character is the dollar character in one of the following forms:
.sp
.ti +2
$$                  insert a dollar character
.br
.ti +2
$n or ${n}          insert the contents of group \fIn\fP
.br
.ti +2
$<name>             insert the contents of named group
.br
.ti +2
$0 or $&            insert the entire matched substring
.br
.ti +2
$`                  insert the substring that precedes the match
.br
.ti +2
$\*(Aq                  insert the substring that follows the match
.br
.ti +2
$_                  insert the entire input string
.br
.ti +2
$+                  insert the highest\-numbered capture group which matched
.br
.ti +2
$*MARK or ${*MARK}  insert a control verb name
.sp
For ${n}, n can be a name or a number. If PCRE2_SUBSTITUTE_EXTENDED is set,
there is additional interpretation:
.sp
1. Backslash is an escape character, and the forms described in "ESCAPED
CHARACTERS" above are recognized. Also:
.sp
.ti +2
\(rsQ...\(rsE can be used to suppress interpretation
.br
.ti +2
\(rsl      force the next character to lower case
.br
.ti +2
\(rsu      force the next character to upper case
.br
.ti +2
\(rsL      force subsequent characters to lower case
.br
.ti +2
\(rsU      force subsequent characters to upper case
.br
.ti +2
\(rsu\(rsL    force next character to upper case, then all lower
.br
.ti +2
\(rsl\(rsU    force next character to lower case, then all upper
.br
.ti +2
\(rsE      end \(rsL or \(rsU case forcing
.br
.ti +2
\(rsb      backspace character (note: as in character class in pattern)
.br
.ti +2
\(rsv      vertical tab character (note: not the same as in a pattern)
.sp
2. The Python form \(rsg<n>, where the angle brackets are part of the syntax and
\fIn\fP is either a group name or a number, is recognized as an alternative way
of inserting the contents of a group, for example \(rsg<3>.
.sp
3. Capture substitution supports the following additional forms:
.sp
.ti +2
${n:\-string}             default for unset group
.br
.ti +2
${n:+string1:string2}    values for set/unset group
.sp
The substitution strings themselves are expanded. Backslash can be used to
escape colons and closing curly brackets.
